    "To fully appreciate what makes V8 unique, one must examine the tension between compilation overhead and peak execution throughput. A pure interpreter like early Python or Ruby starts executing instantly with zero compilation delay because it does not spend time translating source code into machine instructions. However, its peak performance is severely constrained because it must repeatedly parse and dispatch operations in an interpretive loop. Conversely, an ahead-of-time (AOT) optimizing compiler like C++ or Rust requires substantial compilation time up front to analyze control flow and optimize machine code, resulting in stellar runtime throughput. In web browsers and cloud server environments, users demand both instantaneous startup and blistering peak execution speed.",
